finding: P2: Retaining historical membership ordinals introduces change-independent retarget work without demonstrated native-parity need. BodyDependencies.set walks backward over the destination list to insert an old membership; an independently instrumented retarget of one early Shape into a body with 1,000 later Shapes read 1,001 predecessor links. No existing destination Shape changed. This fails the review's change-proportional cost criterion. Box3D b3DestroyBody destroys attached Shapes and joints; it supplies no original-membership-order rule for ECS definitions surviving Body removal, or for migrating a Shape between bodies. FIFO/append dependency ordering fixes the originally reported prepend reversal without historical ordinals; preserving old membership priority through retargets is a stronger policy than that finding established. The ordinal arrays, ordered insertion, candidate heapsort and recovery state need justification against this simpler deterministic rule, not tests asserting the new policy. Normal Node and allocation suites pass; a runtime append/no-sort control still passes the creation-order Node regression and all 600 native/direct/public Rain hashes, while two dependency tests fail their stronger historical-order expectations. This control does not establish parity for every other gold.
